# Accessibility

All production UI must meet modern accessibility expectations.

## Semantic HTML

Use appropriate semantic elements:

- header
- nav
- main
- section
- article
- footer
- button
- form
- label

Do not use divs as interactive controls.

## Keyboard navigation

Every interactive element must be keyboard accessible.

Ensure:

- visible focus
- logical tab order
- escape closes dialogs
- keyboard navigation works in menus

## Forms

Every input must have an accessible label.

Validation errors must be associated with the relevant field.

Do not rely only on red borders.

## Dialogs

Dialogs must:

- trap focus appropriately
- restore focus when closed
- have accessible names
- support Escape

## Images

Every meaningful image requires appropriate alt text.

Decorative images should use empty alt text where appropriate.

## Color

Never communicate information using color alone.

Examples:

Do not use only red to indicate an error.

Use:

- icon
- text
- color

## Motion

Respect:

prefers-reduced-motion

Do not create animations that can negatively affect usability.

## Screen readers

Important state changes should be announced appropriately.

Examples:

- successful cart addition
- form submission failure
- payment status
- destructive action completion

## Accessibility testing

Before completing a feature, inspect:

- keyboard navigation
- focus states
- form labels
- contrast
- screen-reader semantics
- mobile accessibility